title The widest reach yet folded, and the last free row named
prompt Take the next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first, Standfast on reds first, read the council rota row, condense REMEMBER, send the increment
think Standfast first. grep for OPEN in crux/REDS.md found nothing live -- rows %130, %131, and %132 each carry a CLOSED twin -- so the line was clear to open new work.
think Council rota, lap 166, row 166 mod 5 = 1 -- the Air/Saturn triad, law and boundary. context/TAME_CORE.md holds the reflexes; single-stranded names the exact fault this arc removes, since forty-seven copies of one body is a braid laid across forty-seven files; Conway's Law explains why the tree writes everything down, and its inverse is why the fold queue lives inside a witness rather than inside a plan.
think Priced the queue head with sh tools/fixtures/ladder_reach_visibility_scan.sh take_up_again -- declared=14 identical=13 folding=13 below_the_line=0 lines=48 widens=0 stub=3 fall=540. Zero widening, so the lift was free.
think Hashed all fourteen take_up_again bodies. Thirteen matched byte for byte at forty-eight lines; caravan/reopen.rye differed. Diffed it against caravan/abate.rye before touching anything: seven lines, and every one the same substitution -- report_out.taken_up where the thirteen write reopen_of(report_out).taken_up.
think Read reopen.rye's Report to learn whether that was an omission. It is not. reopen.rye declares taken_up, left_closed, and unheeded as its own fields, so reopen_of there would be an identity accessor, saying something other than what reopen_of says in every other rung, which is one tier down through inner. The rung that owns a report reaches its own fields; that is a structural difference and it keeps reopen.rye home.
think Noted this is the second time the arc has met that exact shape -- suffice.rye stays home under weigh_the_answer for the same reason, naming report_out.weighed where its fourteen siblings reach through suffice_of. Two firings, one cause. Recorded in the fold's own doc comment rather than tooled, since the meter already catches it: a rung whose body differs simply never joins the identical set.
think Checked the widening answer by hand before trusting it -- fifteen symbols across thirteen rungs, one grep per pair, all forty-five public. The meter's widens=0 held.
think Lifted the body into caravan/ladder_checks.rye as pub fn take_up_again(comptime rung: type, io: std.Io, reopen: rung.Reopen, report_out: *rung.Report) (rung.RunError || rung.NoteError)!void, prefixing all fifteen reached symbols with the rung type and leaving the two error values bare, since error.ReopenUnweighed and error.ReopenMisrecorded resolve globally. Stubbed thirteen rungs to three lines each, each keeping its own doc comment above the stub.
think Measured after the fold rather than assuming: carry 70,345 -> 69,805, a fall of exactly 540. Lowered the ceiling in tools/fixtures/caravan_ladder_carry_scan.sh and re-pinned both asserts in the carry witness, then rewrote its two say lines to name this lap's fold, keeping every digit spelled in words so the prose-count scan stays satisfied (REDS %131).
think Advanced the reach-visibility witness one place. Its folded slot now reads take_up_again returning fall=0, its head slot read_count at 462 free, and its third slot changed meaning: with read_count folded there are no free rows left, so the slot now pins load_one at 549 for a hundred and twenty widenings -- the largest realized fall remaining, and the first row the arc will have to pay for.
think Ran the choir and the era suite AFTER the last edit rather than before it, since a witness proves the tree it ran on and the previous lap shipped a red by editing a meter's prose after the choir had already sung.
obs Prediction met measurement to the digit for the ninth lap running. Carry 70,345 -> 69,805, a fall of exactly 540; bodies 18,172 -> 18,173; distinct 7,970 -> 7,971; families hold at 703; ceiling down to 69,805.
obs The widest reach the arc has folded. Fifteen symbols is nearly double the previous high, and every one was already public in all thirteen rungs -- so reach width and widening cost are independent, which the carried-lines column could never have shown.
obs The free rows are nearly spent. read_count at 462 is the last zero-widening row on the board; after it every remaining family costs a widening, and the arc's next question stops being mechanical. start_one is the queue's own counterexample: 574 carried lines that return nothing at all against two hundred and ninety-four openings.
think Ran three per-rung witnesses beside the singing choir to get faster feedback, and watched caravan_reweigh_witness.rish and caravan_reopen_witness.rish both go RED. reopen.rye had not been touched at all this lap, which is the tell -- one red on a file the lap edited is a fold to re-examine, a second on a file it never opened is a meter to distrust. Both ran GREEN alone.
think Read tools/caravan_reweigh_witness.rish line 86 and found the cause: it builds caravan/read.rye to the literal path caravan/bin/read, and grep found the same literal in seventy-nine siblings -- two hundred and eight references, the one shared output path in a build tree whose per-rung binaries were already unique. Two processes writing one file race; one truncates while the other links, and rye build answers with a failure indistinguishable from a broken rung.
think Booked REDS %133 and closed it in the same lap: each witness now builds to caravan/bin/<rung>_read, derived from its own filename. caravan/bin/ is gitignored, so only the build tree changed. Named the residue honestly rather than claiming the class closed -- running the SAME witness twice at once still races on that rung's own binary, and no naming scheme can fix that, since both runs genuinely want the same output.
obs REDS %92 lit this same lantern once already. That row taught each witness to build its reader rather than borrow one off disk, and all eighty carry the comment saying so -- yet they all built it to the same name, so the borrowing returned through the output path instead of the input. Owning the build and owning the file it lands in are two halves of one invariant, and only the first half had been written down.
obs One red booked and closed inside the lap. A false RED is worse than a missed one: it teaches a bench that a green tree can look red, so the next real red is met with a shrug. The Radiant pass playbook already writes that sentence for blocking witnesses; it holds for a shared output path too. A meter must be a function of the tree alone, and a path two runs can both hold makes it a function of the schedule as well.
obs The REDS pin passed its 24,576-byte bound as row %133 landed, so rows %129 and %130 folded to crux/archive/REDS-roster-and-queue-column-rows-129-130.md and the headline was re-derived from the spine rather than edited by hand -- rows 133, remainder 113.
